        "The SWIS has a strong pipeline of renewable energy projects in-development, but prioritisation for storage projects, system planning inconsistencies and lack of industrial power purchase agreements (PPAs) have hindered delivery. We therefore score it as 2.",
        "The renewable energy generation required to power Kwinana will be housed in the wheatbelt and mid-west regions of the SWIS. The pipeline of projects in-development in this footprint is substantial, meeting 160% of 2035 regional requirements according to BZE analysis. However, the implementation is lagging. Only 30% of 2035 requirements have received development approval, and just 8% is in construction or operation. Since the end of 2020, only one wind farm and a single solar farm have been constructed – totalling just 205 MW in nameplate capacity<sup class=\"refMark\">1,2</sup>.",
        "A number of factors have contributed to this lag:",
        "<ul class=\"bulletList\"><li>Storage prioritisation in an isolated grid: As the SWIS operates as an isolated system, Synergy – the government-owned gentailer – has prioritised large-scale battery construction in Collie and Kwinana to maintain system security. While necessary, this has directed capital and policy attention toward storage ahead of generation.</li><li>System planning inconsistency: Shifts between the 2020 Whole of System Plan, the 2023 SWIS Demand Assessment, and the 2025 SWIS Transmission Plan have created uncertainty around long-term demand projections and infrastructure sequencing. FESO 2027, the statutory successor to the WOSP, is intended to end this pattern, but will not report until 2027<sup class=\"refMark\">3</sup>.</li><li>Connection process bottlenecks: The former first-come, first-served grid connection model contributed to delays for otherwise mature projects.</li><li>Workforce and contractor capacity: The trades required to build out SWIS renewable generation such as electricians, riggers, civil and industrial contractors are in structural, multi-year national shortage, and are actively drawn away from clean energy projects by wage competition from Western Australia's mining and LNG sectors<sup class=\"refMark\">4</sup>. Entry-level pipelines such as the Key Apprenticeship Program and Free TAFE produce qualified tradespeople on a three-to-four-year lag, limiting how quickly this constraint can ease within the 2026–2030 window in which most approved projects need to reach construction. The delivery implications and policy response are assessed in Sections 3.1.2 and 3.2.2.</li></ul>",
        "Despite these headwinds, there are reasons for optimism. State-level approvals are proceeding quickly, with recent estimates placing median approval times at approximately six months for wind farms and two months for solar farms<sup class=\"refMark\">5</sup>. Since October 2025, the cumulative capacity of projects with development approval has risen by 50%. Western Power has introduced a Critical Projects framework within its connection process to prioritise connection-ready projects<sup class=\"refMark\">6</sup>. In parallel, Synergy is progressing a suite of large-scale projects, including the proposed Tathra Wind and Solar Farms, which secured state-level approvals within four months<sup class=\"refMark\">7</sup>. Synergy and the Water Corporation also signed PPAs to support the development of three other wind farms in the state’s Mid-West and Wheatbelt regions, totalling over 1 GW of generation capacity<sup class=\"refMark\">8</sup>. Delivery of many of these projects, however, is contingent on completion of the Clean Energy Link – North transmission upgrades, required to relieve grid constraints north of Perth<sup class=\"refMark\">9</sup>.",
        "For Kwinana’s renewable energy readiness to improve, a greater share of projects must progress to construction. Grid connection timelines must continue to fall, industrial electrification and green industry strategies should be leveraged to stimulate power purchase agreements and accelerate projects toward final investment decision, and the FESO 2027 must deliver a more transparent and sequenced infrastructure development plan.",
