        "Strategic and nature-positive land-use planning scores a 2. Public data exists for biodiversity, agriculture, and energy infrastructure (for existing and commissioning energy projects), but is generally only available as printable maps rather than downloadable GIS datasets. No combined biodiversity prioritisation layer has been identified, and while research on strategic land use covers Perth and Peel, it doesn't extend to the Wheatbelt and renewable energy implications are unclear. A bilateral assessment agreement between the Federal and Western Australian governments is pending targeted for the end of 2026<sup class=\"refMark\">1</sup>. Western Australia has a planning framework and regional planning effort underway, but lacks a single, spatially-explicit, cross-sectoral land-use plan.",
