        "The April 2026 MoU on EPBC assessment and approval bilaterals<sup class=\"refMark\">10</sup> is a welcome step towards greater resourcing and attention towards biodiversity protection and strategic land use planning for renewable energy and other infrastructure project approvals. How it works in practice is yet to be seen. The public conversation to build consensus around strategic land use planning will also need to be elevated.",
        "WA has a planning framework in place and a substantial integrated regional planning effort underway. However, it doesn't yet have a single, spatially-explicit, cross-sectoral land-use plan. WAs State Planning Strategy 2050 provides strategic context for all planning and development decisions in WA, structured around six principles (Community, Economy, Environment, Infrastructure, Regional Development, and Governance)<sup class=\"refMark\">11</sup>. It's high-level and sets direction, but is not an integrated plan.",
        "Under the State Planning Strategy 2050<sup class=\"refMark\">11</sup> sits the Western Australian Planning Commission’s (WAPC's) state planning policy framework. As part of this framework, in June 2022 the WAPC authorised preparation of eight new Regional Planning Strategies covering Gascoyne, Goldfields-Esperance, Great Southern, Kimberley, Mid West, Pilbara, South West and Wheatbelt.These strategies are intended to \"define and shape appropriate planning responses over the next 20 to 30 years\"<sup class=\"refMark\">12</sup>. However, the publicly available material is dated and, at the time of this assessment in July 2026, there was no evidence that any of the eight had been published as final documents.",
        "Two other planning instruments of note are the draft Renewable Energy Planning Code (DREP) and Infrastructure WA's State Infrastructure Strategy 2022. The draft code was released by the WAPC for consultation from 12 December 2025 to 10 April 2026, with accompanying Guidelines<sup class=\"refMark\">13</sup>. It is an assessment code, not a strategic siting product. Additionally, WA does not have a Renewable Energy Zone (REZ) framework.",
