        "The score of 3 reflects a moderate or “developing” level of community benefits, however this assessment found that community benefits and impacts were inconsistent and fragmented; and that communities supporting the SWIS to adopt renewable energy are not benefiting in an even and consistent way. Some communities are benefiting significantly and others missing out. The implementation of the Community Benefits Guidelines is an intervention that is likely to remedy the disparity.",
        "Nuance is needed to interpret the dimensions of this indicator. Overall, the employment dimension dragged the score up:",
        "3.7 - Economic benefits employment: Renewable energy projects tended to score higher in this dimension, as construction, operations and maintenance does bring jobs.",
        "2.7 Other economic benefits: Scoring starts to lack consistency in this dimension, with some projects offering best practice standards and others with minimal additional benefits.",
        "2.8 Specific Community Benefit Funding Arrangements: Most projects have some kind of community benefit fund, however some have none at all.",
        "2.8 Community Lead Benefit Management: The Collie community has had community and local, state and federal government discussions and planning around the region’s economic future due to the planned closure of the coal fired power stations. This appears to have influenced nearby regions that have more proactively sought benefits from renewable energy while other regions had no local benefit plans.",
        "Overall, our analysis found net positive impacts and benefits and also examples of very poor practice and no benefit - and that this can even occur within the same LGA. The uneven distribution of benefits and lack of specific benefit funds found in some communities show that the Powering WA Guidelines<sup class=\"refMark\">2</sup> are a timely and much needed intervention. While the Guidelines aren’t mandatory, they provide clear standards on expected benefit contributions and give communities leverage to articulate what’s expected.",
        "An exciting aspect of the guidelines are the pilots for local economic development plans to be created by local community groups to deliver place based outcomes with the benefit funds. Powering WA is appointing independent agencies to work across the communities to help develop these plans, and funding generated from renewable energy contributions will be administered by the Regional Development Commissions, that are also located in place.",
